winemac: give D3DMetal a hosted swapchain for a window it does not own.

D3DMetal drives this driver through the function table d3dmetal.c exports. For a new
swapchain it calls get_win_data(), reads client_cocoa_view out of the record, and hands
that view to view_create_metal_view() and view_get_metal_layer(); libd3dshared's
WineSwapchainCallbacks::InitializeForHWND makes exactly those calls and no others. It
does not check the record for NULL. A window another process owns has no record in this
process, so a Chromium GPU process asking for a swapchain on its browser's window read
offset 0x18 of NULL and died with ACCESS_VIOLATION: Steam's client, three times per
start, and then a software fallback this driver cannot show either. Measured in sake on
2026-09-20 with WINEDEBUG=+macdrv_d3dmtl,+seh,+win and a vmmap of the GPU process;
docs/runtime.md has the numbers.

The shim cannot be changed, so the glue keeps its side of the contract and changes what
it hands over. A window that exists but is not ours now gets a record whose view is the
client surface's, remembered in a table; view_create_metal_view() on that view acquires
the swapchain that 0004 and 0005 host in the owner's window and hands back a view made
for it, view_get_metal_layer() answers that view with the hosted layer, and
view_release_metal_view() releases both. A window this process owns takes the same path
it always did.

The view has to be a real NSView. The first version of this patch returned the client
surface itself as the "metal view" and D3DMetal died in objc_msgSend_stret with the
surface as receiver: it asks the view for its bounds before it touches the layer.
WineHostedMetalView is that view, sized like the window and attached to nothing, because
nothing in this process has the window.

Two assumptions this rests on, both to be read off a run: the hosted layer's device is
the system default, as D3DMetal's is, and D3DMetal accepts a record with no
cocoa_window, which a child window of ours already gives it. If either fails the shim
logs "Failed to get drawable for HWND" and the window stays black; the TRACEs on the
macdrv_d3dmtl channel say how far the six calls got.

--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
@@ -4375,7 +4375,30 @@
 {
     [(id<WineMetalSwapChain>)swapchain release];
 }
+
 
+/* A view for a swapchain hosted in another process's window. D3DMetal treats what
+ * view_create_metal_view() returns as an NSView -- it asks it for its bounds through
+ * objc_msgSend_stret before it ever touches the layer -- so the hosted layer is given a
+ * view of its own. The view sits in no window, because nothing in this process has one
+ * for it, and it carries no layer: the layer comes from the swapchain, not from AppKit. */
+@interface WineHostedMetalView : NSView
+@end
+
+@implementation WineHostedMetalView
+@end
+
+macdrv_metal_view macdrv_create_hosted_metal_view(CGRect frame)
+{
+    __block WineHostedMetalView* view;
+
+    OnMainThread(^{
+        view = [[WineHostedMetalView alloc] initWithFrame:NSRectFromCGRect(frame)];
+    });
+
+    return (macdrv_metal_view)view;
+}
+
 void macdrv_window_create_ca_layer_host_view(macdrv_window w, unsigned int context_id,
                                              CGRect frame, double zpos, int zpos_valid)
 {
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_cocoa.h
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_cocoa.h
@@ -565,6 +565,7 @@
